// `union` and `intersect` taking any number of sets, 2026-10-22, in `merge`'s shape: the immutable
// member has no distinguished receiver, so `union(a, b, c)` and `union(sets)` are the same call and
// folding a list needs no loop at the call site. Both are symmetric, which is what makes a receiver
// pointless for them -- `difference` keeps one because it is not.
//
// **The two in-place members stay distinguishable only because of `#[at_least_one]`.** They differ
// in the *element type* of their variadic tail, `Set` against `MutableSet`, and nothing else; with
// a zero-length tail allowed, `union(s)` would match both with no argument to choose between them.
// The attribute is part of the arity and so part of distinguishability, which is the load-bearing
// use of it rather than a decorative one.
//
// **A tail comes from one world.** `union(target, aSet, aMutableSet)` is a type error naming
// argument 3, because a parameter cannot be both -- the wall `concat` and `merge` document.
//
// **`union` of none is the empty set; `intersect` of none is a runtime error.** The asymmetry is
// the mathematics: union's identity is the empty set and exists, intersection's is the universal
// set and does not. `concat()` succeeds for the same reason `union` does, and `min`/`max` of an
// empty array raise for the same reason `intersect` does. `#[at_least_one]` rules out a call with
// no argument at all; an empty array *spread* is one argument and reaches the function, which is
// exactly the hole that segfaulted `merge` on the same day.
fn main() {
    val a = to_set([1, 2, 3])
    val b = to_set([3, 4])
    val c = to_set([3, 9])

    // one, two, three, and a spread list -- all the same name
    print(to_array(union(a)))
    print(to_array(union(a, b)))
    print(to_array(union(a, b, c)))
    print(to_array(union([a, b, c])))

    print(to_array(intersect(a)))
    print(to_array(intersect(a, b, c)))
    print(to_array(intersect([a, b, c])))

    // union of nothing is the empty set
    val no_sets: [Set<int>] = []
    print(len(union(no_sets)))

    // in-place, with Set operands and then with MutableSet operands
    val m1 = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(m1, 1, 2, 3)
    union(m1, b, c)
    intersect(m1, to_set([2, 3, 9]))
    print(to_array(m1))

    val ob = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(ob, 3, 4)
    val oc = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(oc, 3, 9)
    val m2 = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(m2, 1, 2, 3)
    union(m2, ob, oc)
    print(to_array(m2))

    val m3 = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(m3, 1, 2, 3)
    intersect(m3, ob, oc)
    print(to_array(m3))

    // an empty operand tail is a no-op, which is what naming only the receiver means
    val m4 = mutable_set_new<int>()
    add(m4, 7)
    union(m4, no_sets)
    intersect(m4, no_sets)
    print(to_array(m4))
}
